"""Convention followed: **Controller pattern** — stateless HTTP adapters;
handlers stay thin: parse the request, delegate to ``self.repo``,
serialize the answer. Every handler returns ``Result[T, E]``
(Convention: **Result + ProblemDetail**): the repository returns blocked
outcomes as ``Err`` values (R6: absence is a value, never ``None``), the
controller maps the ``Ok`` arm through to a body, and the Result bridge
renders any ``Err`` as RFC-9457 ``ProblemDetail``. A handler never probes
a call result (``is None``, truthiness) — one representation per outcome.
Even the normally infallible ``create``/``list`` repository calls are
Result-shaped, so one HTTP verb set has exactly one return shape.

{{ class_name }} controller module.
{% if doc %}
{{ doc }}
{% endif %}
Scaffold: endpoints delegate to ``self.repo`` — wire a repository with
CRUD methods ({{ ops_enabled | join('/') }}) in your DI provider.
{% if ops['create'] or ops['update'] %}
Request bodies are validated through the generated Pydantic DTO{% if ops['create'] and ops['update'] %}s{% endif %}
({% if ops['create'] %}``{{ class_name }}Create``{% endif %}{% if ops['create'] and ops['update'] %} / {% endif %}{% if ops['update'] %}``{{ class_name }}Update``{% endif %}) so field types and
required-ness are enforced before the repository sees them; a body that
is malformed or fails validation answers 400 as
``Err(Invalid{{ class_name }}Error)``.
{% if principal_fields %}Ownership fields ({{ principal_fields | join(', ') }}) are assigned from the
authenticated principal on create — they are absent from the write DTOs, so
a request body can never spoof them.
{% endif %}{% if validator_module %}The wired validator (``{{ validator_function }}``) is enforced after DTO
validation on create/update; a payload breaking a declared rule answers 422
before any repository write.
{% endif %}{% endif %}"""
